<h1>Pantry Management &amp; QR Ordering</h1>
<p class="story-lead">A global logistics leader’s pantry was flooded with 300+ phone orders a day, and many arrived late. FusionEdge replaced the calls with a QR scan, added automatic escalation for late orders, and set up a different menu for each meeting room.</p>
<ul>
<li>1,200+ employees served</li>
<li>6 pantries across 4 floors, and 18 meeting rooms on 3 levels</li>
<li>450+ pantry orders a day</li>
<li>Average delivery time: 22 minutes to 8 minutes</li>
<li>Orders delivered on time: 68% to 97%</li>
<li>Escalations to management: 40 a month to 3</li>
<li>Phone calls to the pantry: 300+ a day to almost zero</li>
<li>Employee satisfaction: 3.1 to 4.6 out of 5</li>
<li>Rollout time: 3 weeks</li>
</ul>
<h2>The client and the challenge</h2>
<p>The client is one of the world’s leading global logistics companies. One office of more than 1,200 employees has pantries that serve tea, coffee, snacks, and meeting refreshments all day. Every order came in by phone. With hundreds of calls a day, the pantry team could not keep up.</p>
<ul>
<li>Calls were missed, or orders were noted down wrong.</li>
<li>Items reached desks and meeting rooms late, sometimes after the meeting had ended.</li>
<li>Nobody could see which order was pending, or for how long.</li>
<li>Unhappy employees complained straight to senior managers.</li>
<li>Client meetings and internal meetings got the same service, with no difference in what was offered.</li>
</ul>
<p>The result was a poor employee experience and an embarrassing gap in front of visiting clients.</p>
<h2>What we found</h2>
<p>Before rollout, FusionEdge studied two weeks of pantry activity.</p>
<ul>
<li>300+ phone orders to the pantry each day. The team spent most of its time on calls.</li>
<li>32% of orders delivered late. Almost 1 in 3 missed its time.</li>
<li>Average delivery time of 22 minutes. A cup of tea took longer than a short meeting.</li>
<li>40 escalations to management each month.</li>
<li>0% of orders had any written record, so there was no data to plan stock or staffing.</li>
</ul>
<h2>The solution</h2>
<p>FusionEdge Pantry Management went live across all 6 pantries and 18 meeting rooms in 3 weeks, in four steps.</p>
<ul>
<li>Scan a QR code to order. Codes were placed at every pantry, desk area, and meeting room. Employees scan with their phone, pick items from a menu, and tap order. No app download, and no phone call.</li>
<li>Automatic escalation if an order is late. Every order has a timer. If it is not delivered on time, FusionEdge alerts the right person.</li>
<li>Pre-schedule orders. Employees can book refreshments in advance, for example coffee for a 3 pm meeting, so the pantry can prepare ahead.</li>
<li>A menu for every meeting room level. The QR menu changes based on the room it is scanned in. Internal meetings see beverages only. Client meetings get a wider choice.</li>
</ul>
<h2>How escalation works</h2>
<ul>
<li>At the due time, pantry staff are alerted to deliver now.</li>
<li>5 minutes late, the pantry supervisor steps in and clears the delay.</li>
<li>10 minutes late, the facility head is told about the impact on the employee experience.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Menus by meeting room</h2>
<ul>
<li>Level 1, team rooms, for internal meetings: beverages only, tea, coffee, and water.</li>
<li>Level 2, conference rooms, for reviews and cross-team meetings: beverages and light snacks.</li>
<li>Level 3, client and board rooms: the full menu, with beverages, snacks, fruit platters, and working lunches.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Before and after</h2>
<ul>
<li>How orders are placed: phone calls, 300+ a day, replaced by a QR scan in under 30 seconds.</li>
<li>Delivery time: 22 minutes on average, then 8 minutes.</li>
<li>On-time delivery: 68%, then 97%.</li>
<li>Late orders: noticed only when someone complained, then auto-escalated at 5 and 10 minutes.</li>
<li>Escalations to management: 40 a month, then 3 a month.</li>
<li>Meeting refreshments: the same for every meeting, then a menu matched to the room level and meeting type.</li>
<li>Planning: no order records, then every order logged, with peak hours and top items visible.</li>
</ul>
<p>With every order recorded, the facility team now stocks the pantry based on real demand, which cut wastage of milk, snacks, and fruit by about 25%.</p>
<h2>The five outcomes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Faster service. Average delivery time down from 22 to 8 minutes, with 97% of orders on time.</li>
<li>Accountability. Late orders are escalated automatically at 5 and 10 minutes. Escalations to management are down 92%.</li>
<li>Better experience. Employee satisfaction up from 3.1 to 4.6 out of 5, and clients get the right refreshments every time.</li>
<li>Standardization. One ordering process and one menu structure across 6 pantries and 18 meeting rooms.</li>
<li>Smarter planning. Pre-scheduled orders and order data help plan stock and staff, with about 25% less wastage.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Modules used</h2>
<ul>
<li>Pantry Management, for QR ordering, menus by room level, and pre-scheduled orders</li>
<li>Smart Ticketing, for timers and automatic escalations on late orders</li>
<li>Executive Dashboard, for a live view of orders, delivery times, and pantry performance</li>
</ul>
